04:06.100 --> 04:08.240
Wyckoff schematics are not fractals.

04:08.600 --> 04:09.660
This is not what this is for.

04:09.820 --> 04:13.100
They need to have a matching fingerprint volume profile.

04:13.520 --> 04:15.820
Otherwise, the pattern is not valid, okay?

04:15.880 --> 04:19.480
And you can get something like this show up that resolves downwards.

04:19.480 --> 04:20.900
And why is that?

04:20.980 --> 04:24.660
It will be because we do not have the matching fingerprint and the volume profile, okay?

04:24.720 --> 04:27.500
If it's accumulation, we need to see absorption at the lows.

04:27.880 --> 04:29.560
So here's a low, here's absorption.

04:29.960 --> 04:32.120
If it was more distributive in nature, okay?

04:32.160 --> 04:35.040
So we get a run-up, a Wyckoff distribution, and then a mark down.

04:35.380 --> 04:39.380
You would expect to see the institutions moving large volume into the highs.

04:39.620 --> 04:43.440
And if you don't have either of those, or if you have no volume whatsoever, okay?

04:43.500 --> 04:45.300
Then the pattern is simply invalid.

04:45.500 --> 04:47.380
That's how it works, because it's not a fractal.

04:47.380 --> 04:49.800
It's not designed for you to just overlay this and say,

04:49.880 --> 04:51.760
oh, this is probably what's happening here, right?

04:52.180 --> 04:53.860
But very few people seem to know that.

04:54.260 --> 04:58.080
Anyway, shout out Hot Potato Trader for including a volume profile here.

